<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Recognizing the cost of equity-settled and cash-settled employee compensation</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3870 governs the RECOGNITION and MEASUREMENT of STOCK-based compensation and OTHER stock-BASED payments — AWARDS of SHARES, share OPTIONS, or OTHER equity INSTRUMENTS granted TO employees or OTHERS in EXCHANGE for GOODS or SERVICES. THE Section INCLUDES a PRACTICAL accommodation RELEVANT specifically TO private ENTERPRISES lacking a READILY observable MARKET price FOR their OWN equity (Section 6).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Equity-settled and cash-settled stock-based arrangements</div><div class="principle">This Section applies to STOCK options, STOCK appreciation RIGHTS, restricted STOCK, and OTHER arrangements UNDER which an ENTERPRISE compensates EMPLOYEES or OTHERS through EQUITY instruments OR payments BASED on the VALUE of ITS equity.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The General Approach — the Fair Value Based Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Measuring compensation cost at the grant-date fair value</div><div class="text-supporting">COMPENSATION cost is GENERALLY measured AT the FAIR value OF the EQUITY instruments GRANTED, DETERMINED using an OPTION-pricing model (e.g. BLACK-Scholes) OR OTHER appropriate VALUATION technique — BROADLY consistent WITH the GENERAL fair-VALUE-based approach ALSO used INTERNATIONALLY.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Recognition Over the Vesting Period</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Expense spread over the period services are rendered</div><div class="text-supporting">COMPENSATION cost is RECOGNIZED over the VESTING period — the PERIOD during WHICH the EMPLOYEE must PROVIDE service BEFORE the AWARD becomes UNCONDITIONALLY the EMPLOYEE'S own — REFLECTING the FACT that the AWARD compensates FOR service RENDERED over THAT entire PERIOD, RATHER than SOLELY at the GRANT date.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Awards to Non-Employees</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Measured at the fair value of goods or services received, where more reliable</div><div class="text-supporting">FOR awards GRANTED to NON-employees (e.g. CONSULTANTS or OTHER service PROVIDERS), the TRANSACTION is MEASURED at the FAIR value OF the GOODS or SERVICES received, WHERE that IS more RELIABLY measurable THAN the fair VALUE of the EQUITY instruments GRANTED.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>A Practical Accommodation for Enterprises Without Observable Equity Values</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Addressing the genuine practical challenge private enterprises face</div><div class="text-supporting">MANY private ENTERPRISES lack a READILY determinable MARKET price FOR their OWN equity INSTRUMENTS — MAKING application OF a STANDARD option-PRICING model GENUINELY more DIFFICULT than FOR a PUBLICLY traded ENTITY with an OBSERVABLE share PRICE. ASPE addresses THIS practical CHALLENGE by ALLOWING closely-HELD enterprises, IN appropriate CIRCUMSTANCES, to APPLY simplified OR alternative valuation TECHNIQUES (SUCH as an INTRINSIC-value-based approach, MEASURING compensation COST based on the DIFFERENCE between an APPRAISED or ESTIMATED value OF the underlying STOCK and the EXERCISE price) WHERE a MORE sophisticated OPTION-pricing model WOULD otherwise be IMPRACTICABLE to APPLY reliably — a GENUINELY practical ACCOMMODATION consistent WITH the BROADER cost-CONSCIOUS philosophy RUNNING throughout ASPE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Forfeitures and Modifications</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Reversing expense for forfeited awards, incremental cost for modifications</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE an AWARD is FORFEITED before VESTING (e.g. an EMPLOYEE leaves), PREVIOUSLY recognized COMPENSATION cost is REVERSED. WHERE an AWARD'S terms ARE subsequently MODIFIED in a WAY that INCREASES its FAIR value, the INCREMENTAL fair VALUE is RECOGNIZED as ADDITIONAL compensation COST, over the REMAINING vesting PERIOD.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Stock Appreciation Rights and Other Cash-Settled Awards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A liability, remeasured until settlement</div><div class="text-supporting">FOR cash-SETTLED awards (SUCH as stock APPRECIATION rights, ENTITLING the HOLDER to a CASH payment BASED on the INCREASE in the ENTERPRISE'S own SHARE value), a LIABILITY is RECOGNIZED and REMEASURED at EACH reporting DATE until SETTLEMENT, WITH changes in FAIR value recognized WITHIN compensation COST.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Expensing an Option Grant Under the Fair Value Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Spreading grant-date fair value over the vesting period</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Options granted with a 4-year vesting period</div><div class="wk-body">A company grants employee stock options with a total grant-date fair value of $480,000, vesting evenly over 4 years of continued service.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes COMPENSATION expense OF $480,000 ÷ 4 = <strong>$120,000 per year</strong>, over the FOUR-year vesting PERIOD, REFLECTING the SERVICE being RENDERED throughout THAT period.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example — a Cash-Settled Stock Appreciation Right</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A liability remeasured each period until settlement</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A stock appreciation right granted to a key executive</div><div class="wk-body">A company grants a stock appreciation right to an executive, with a fair value of $60,000 at the current reporting date, up from $45,000 at the prior reporting date, fully vested.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes ADDITIONAL compensation EXPENSE of $60,000 − $45,000 = <strong>$15,000</strong> for the PERIOD, INCREASING the RECOGNIZED liability TO $60,000, REMEASURED again AT the NEXT reporting DATE until the RIGHT is ULTIMATELY settled.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3870 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Description of stock-based compensation arrangements, including vesting conditions</span><span>Compensation cost recognized during the period, and the method used to determine fair value</span><span>Number and weighted average exercise price of options outstanding, granted, exercised, and forfeited</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Which valuation technique is appropriate, particularly for closely held enterprises without observable equity values</span><span>Expected forfeiture rates</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Grant-date fair value, using an option-pricing model or alternative valuation technique</span><span>Fair value of cash-settled awards at each subsequent reporting date</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS 2</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASPE Section 3870 vs IFRS 2</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3870</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 2</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>General fair value based method</span><span>YES</span><span>YES — converged</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Simplified valuation for closely held enterprises</span><span>Available in appropriate circumstances</span><span>Narrower practical expedients only</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">BROADLY converged IN overall APPROACH, with ASPE'S own PRACTICAL accommodation FOR enterprises LACKING observable EQUITY values BEING the MOST notable, THOUGH relatively NARROW, point OF divergence.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every stock-based compensation event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Equity-settled award expense</span><span><span class="badge is">IS</span> <span class="badge sre">SRE</span></span><span>Recognized over the vesting period, credited to contributed surplus</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cash-settled award remeasurement</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Liability remeasured each period until settlement</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3251</span><span>Equity — governs the contributed surplus credit for equity-settled stock-based compensation</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Vesting period</div><div class="glossary-def">The period during which an employee must provide service before a stock-based award becomes unconditionally theirs.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Stock appreciation right</div><div class="glossary-def">A cash-settled award entitling the holder to a payment based on the increase in an enterprise's share value, recognized as a liability remeasured until settlement.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3870 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
